CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ reachable state before try: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
found no match for symbol 'Ex'
--
N5008 [except.handle]/1-3: a `catch(T &e)` / `catch(T e)` handler declares the
exception variable without synthesising a spurious constructor.  CBMC
initialized the catch variable with an int 0 placeholder, which for a
class-typed catch variable made the type-checker construct the class from an
int ("found no match for symbol 'Ex' ... argument types: signed int") and drop
it with a CONVERSION ERROR -- failing for every class type without a matching
int constructor, including move-only classes.  Fixed by nondet-initializing the
catch variable of any type (cpp_typecheck_code.cpp / cpp_typecheck_initializer).

Root cause of the goto-cc compilation failure of CBMC's own parse_options.cpp.
Non-vacuous via the reachable assertions before the try; CBMC does not yet
propagate the thrown value into the handler, so the caught value itself is not
asserted here.
